When no ref is passed: `forwardedRef` is null → `mergedProps.ref = slottableElementRef`. Note: when the child has no ref, `slottableElementRef` can be `undefined`. In that case, `mergedProps.ref = undefined` — an explicit `ref: undefined` prop is passed to `cloneElement`. In React 19, `element.props.ref` is a normal prop… setting `ref: undefined` on the props of a cloned element — the behavior is the same as not setting it (ref is undefined either way). So the child's own ref (when present) is preserved: `mergedProps` spreads `slottableElement.props` and then overrides with `ref: slottableElementRef` (the child's own ref).

After the fix: identical logic — `mergedProps.ref = forwardedRef ? composedRef : slottableElementRef;` — same expression when `forwardedRef` is null. The only behavioral difference: the memoized closure of `composedRef` is created (via the hook) on every render, but is not attached when there is no forwardedRef. So the ref behavior when there is no forwardedRef is identical: the child's own ref is attached, and no composed callback is injected.

Wait — one subtle difference: before the fix, `composeRefs(forwardedRef, slottableElementRef)` was created every render regardless, but not used when there's no forwardedRef. No behavior difference.

Another subtle point: when there is no forwardedRef and the child has no ref either → `mergedProps.ref = undefined` both before and after the fix.

